{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"[description-trim 2026-08-30] Moved investigation narration out of the description to keep it under the plan-review oversize admission limit (=8000 chars). Preserved here verbatim:\n\n## Root cause detail (SDK-level, proven Q1 spike)\nThe `-32001` is a client-side SDK timeout (TS SDK `DEFAULT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MSEC=60000`, `resetTimeoutOnProgress=false`; Python SDK a single `anyio.fail_after` with no progress reset). rebar's server runs the gate under `anyio.fail_after(None)`, so it COMPLETES and signs after the client has already given up — hence keepalive/progress alone cannot be the primary fix, and the durable verdict already exists server-side. rebar already ships the correct app-level answer: `run_workflow` (`_mcp_writes.py`) returns a `{run_id,status:'running'}` handle in ms on a background daemon thread and persists to the git-backed event log, polled by `get_workflow_status`/`get_workflow_result`; `plan_review_status` (`_mcp_reads.py`) is already a no-LLM/no-network poll of durable attestation state.\n\n## Poll status contract — full per-state semantics (source: gate_runs.py `gate_run_status`)\n- `running` — the job's daemon is alive (or within the stale grace window); keep polling.\n- `passed`/`failed` — terminal; recorded verdict settled. Carry `durable` (the gate's signed-attestation currency) so the caller can confirm the verdict persisted.\n- `stale-running` — index still reads `running` but no live daemon owns the job past the grace window (crashed leader); non-signalling terminal — safe to re-`*_start`.\n- `attaching` — NON-TERMINAL. Job in flight in the live registry but no index record yet. A follower `*_start` shares the leader's `job_id` and writes no index entry, so a poll in the window before the leader's own `running` write lands would otherwise read a misleading `unknown`. Semantics: keep polling; never treat as missing/terminal.\n- `unknown` — no index record and the registry does not know the `job_id` (unrecognised/never-issued handle, or a durable-read failure surfaced as an explicit `verdict:unknown` rather than a dropped field); terminal-for-this-handle — re-`*_start` for a fresh job.\n"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788217965999545001,"uuid":"7d019fea-c90f-40a2-a196-a6d53fa643e6"}